  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 444 PM EDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nThere is generally good agreement among long term ensemble guidance \nthat ridging over the western Atlantic will continue to strengthen \nand shift closer to the Florida Peninsula. With 500mb heights \nclimbing to near 594 dam over South Florida, temperatures will \nremain quite warm, with highs in the low to mid 90s. Heat indices \nwill also be flirting with Heat Advisory criteria, once again \nbetween 105 and 110 F. This will lead to widespread Moderate \nHeatRisk and isolated Major HeatRisk across South Florida, affecting \nanyone without proper cooling and hydration. \n\nAt the surface, the pressure gradient associated with high pressure \noff the coast of the Carolinas will continue tighten. This will \nresult in continued breezy easterly winds for Friday, with gusts of \n15 to 25 kts possible. Guidance is also hinting at a weak inverted \ntrough approaching from the northern Caribbean on Friday, which \nalso looks to bring some increased moisture to South Florida. \nEnsemble probabilities indicate a high chance (80%) for PWATs to \nexceed 2 inches, above the 75th percentile for this time of year. As \na result, PoPs will be much higher areawide on Friday, with highest \nchances of 90% along interior and Southwest Florida. There is still \nsome uncertainty among guidance on how different solutions resolve \nthe speed and strength of this disturbance, but the added forcing \nfrom this feature should allow for more numerous and stronger storms \nto develop during the afternoon. The main threats will be heavy \nrainfall, gusty winds, and frequent lightning. \n\nFor the weekend and beyond, pressure gradients across the Atlantic \nlook to relax, which will lessen the speed of easterlies across the \nregion. Moisture also looks to trend down to near normal, which \nshould result in more typical sea-breeze induced afternoon \nconvection. However, there is a signal that some drier air and \nSaharan dust may start to work its way into the region by the \nbeginning of next week. Prior to the arrival of the main Saharan Air \nLayer (SAL), a bit of increased mid level dry air may serve to \nenhance the threat of strong to damaging wind gusts from afternoon \nthunderstorms. If conditions get too dry aloft, it is more likely\nthat convection will struggle to develop, decreasing chances for \nstorms. For now, PoPs will be on the lower end to begin next week,\npeaking at around 50% along interior South Florida.",
